<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">César Milstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">César Milstein was an Argentine biochemist who worked mainly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. With Georges Köhler he developed in 1975 the hybridoma technique for producing monoclonal antibodies, now basic tools of medical diagnosis and treatment. He shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cesar-milstein.jpg" alt="César Milstein" width="161" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: original file: Diario La Nueva Provincia de Bahía Blanca; derivative work by Rexmania · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Milstein_lnp_restauraci%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
